Additional isolated Firefox host-failure acceptance used the same
clock-and-storage diagnostic module. After creating a valid slot, three
independent launches injected a storage getter SecurityError, an absent
storage object, and a setItem QuotaExceededError. Each returned failure
on both startup and explicit invocation without replacing the valid
slot. A subsequent unmodified reload succeeded. A separate fresh
profile with Firefox's dom.storage.enabled preference set to false
returned no local storage object, and the diagnostic returned failure.
All six acceptance indicators were green. The injected exception tests
do not measure a real browser quota or establish crash durability.

The native title now executes its existing interactive loop as a sequence
of bounded steps. Each step drains at most one admitted event batch,
advances the same fixed-step simulation, updates preferences and audio,
and submits at most one frame. The last static presentation survives
between calls, quit remains terminal, and the caller selects the event
wait. Native idle waiting and uncapped gameplay remain unchanged.
This is a preparation for callback-driven hosts, not an asynchronous
scene lifetime or a browser runtime.

Fresh validation passed 1,870 aggregate test executions. Two visible
Xvfb/X11 OpenGL runs of the rebuilt 3,589,328-byte release used the
configured external asset catalogue and disposable settings. One
entered all four seats, resized a Fill/touch scene from 960x912 to
900x600 and 520x760, and exited through the pause menu. Of 2,799
